  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through Thursday)\nIssued at 1208 AM CDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nSunday is looking to be the main brunt of the rain for the \ncombined OK/TX Panhandles as the aforementioned trough in the \nshort term cycles through another closed low near the Four Corners\nRegion. Although there may be some disappointment to amounts\nacross central to eastern portions of the combined Panhandles. By\nSunday, PoPs in the western half of the combined Panhandles bump \nup in the 70-90% range with the eastern Panhandles gaining a \n30-70% chance for rain. Some of the highest PoPs remain in the \nSunday to Sunday night time frame for the central to western \nPanhandles. For the eastern Panhandles, especially the \nsoutheastern Panhandles, some of the highest PoPs exist Monday \ninto Monday night. However, by this time showers and \nthunderstorms are likely to have lower rain rates and be much \nlighter than what the western Panhandles will see Sun into Sun\nnight. By Mon night negative H7 theta-e advection is progged to \nmove into the northwest Panhandles shutting down the rain for this\nregion. \n\nOverall, progged instability remains low in the 200-500 J/Kg \nrange. Severe thunderstorms are not anticipated. However, with \njust enough instability combined with PWATs around an 1\" (close to\n200% of normal for this time of year), excessive rainfall cannot \nbe ruled out, especially for localized areas that get multiple \nrounds. Although, the best place to see multiple rounds and more \nthan an inch of storm total rainfall will be the far northwest, \nwhere shower and thunderstorm activity is expected to linger a bit\nlonger. Tonight, an area from Texline to Boise City to Kenton are\noutlooked for a 5% chance of exceeding flash flood guidance. \nTomorrow, from Adrian to Dalhart up into eastern Cimarron County \nwestward are outlooked for a at least a 15% chance to see rainfall\nexceed flash flood guidance. On Sunday that 15% chance \nencompassed about four fifths of the FA excluding the southeastern\ncombined Panhandles. It does not look like rainfall rates will \nexceed 1\" an hour, but if they do it will be with very isolated \nupdrafts. Don't expect widespread excessive rainfall, but be \nprepared as some narrow corridors may still see impacts due to \ntraining storms, especially in the northwestern Panhandles. \n\nThe system is not only looking to bring rain chances, but cooler \ntemperatures as well. From Sunday onward highs will mostly be \nstuck in the 60s, even after the system exits the Southern Plains.\nTuesday through Thursday are looking mainly dry at this time. By \nThursday night, guidance is hinting at another shot for showers \nand thunderstorms as southwest flow aloft returns ahead of another\ntrough. Likely a weaker system, but still drawing in Pacific \nmoisture for rain chances.",
